  "hints": [
    "Partitioning is not sorting; keep the encounter order within each group.",
    "Append nodes to two temporary chains with tail pointers, then connect the chains.",
    "Save the next pointer, detach the node, append to the <x or >=x tail, and continue with the saved pointer. Join the low chain to the high chain at the end.",
    "Detaching nodes prevents stale next pointers from creating a cycle or dragging nodes into the wrong group."
  ],
  "editorial": "Maintain sentinel heads and tails for two chains. Detach each visited node and append it to the appropriate tail. Appending, rather than prepending, preserves order. Joining the low tail to the high head handles empty groups naturally through the sentinels. The reference reconnects all original nodes exactly once without modifying their values; the judge checks node reuse as well as the output values. The oracle uses a stable sort on a Boolean group key, independently of pointer operations.",
